WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.601
Define the service before designing the cluster.

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:10.169
Identify who will use it, which applications will call it, and when demand occurs.

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:16.736
Ten analysts using interactive chat create a different load from an overnight document-processing pipeline.

00:00:16.736 --> 00:00:23.303
Inventory model types, quantizations, context lengths, embedding workloads, and any vision or multimodal processing.

00:00:23.303 --> 00:00:28.599
Each combination has distinct VRAM, system memory, storage, and acceleration requirements.

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:30.930
Separate latency from throughput.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:38.344
Latency is the time a user waits for useful output, including queueing, prompt processing, and generation.

00:00:38.344 --> 00:00:42.793
Throughput is the amount of work completed over time.

00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:48.937
Batching may improve total throughput while increasing the wait for an individual request.

00:00:48.937 --> 00:00:57.199
Define targets such as acceptable time to first token, requests per minute, concurrent sessions, and maximum queue depth.

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:03.766
Use representative prompts because short synthetic tests can hide the cost of long contexts.

00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:06.944
Document privacy, uptime, and recovery expectations.

00:01:06.944 --> 00:01:13.511
Determine where prompts, retrieved documents, model outputs, and logs may be stored or transmitted.

00:01:13.511 --> 00:01:23.044
Decide whether planned maintenance is acceptable, whether a failed node must be bypassed automatically, and how long service restoration may take.

00:01:23.044 --> 00:01:32.577
Convert budget into more than purchase price: include network equipment, storage, support time, software, power, cooling, rack space, and expected utilization.

00:01:32.577 --> 00:01:35.331
Finally, challenge the cluster assumption.

00:01:35.331 --> 00:01:44.864
A larger single server, scheduled access to one workstation, or a managed service may satisfy the requirement with less operational burden.

00:01:44.864 --> 00:01:53.550
Build a cluster when measurements show a need for independent model roles, aggregate concurrency, fault isolation, or horizontal growth.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:02:02.659
Requirements protect the project from solving an imagined scale problem while overlooking the cost of operating a real distributed system.
